+# Design: Move off request
+
+Ecosystem: JavaScript (CommonJS, Node >= 18). Current version in use: request 2.88.2 (latest is 2.88.2).
+
+## Approach
+
+Replace the two `request` calls in `src/rates.js` with the global `fetch`. No package is added to `dependencies`.
+`fast-check` is added as a `devDependency` for the séance; the project's runner stays `node --test`.
+
+## The séance (differential test)
+
+`test/seance.request.test.js`, named `séance: request vs fetch`, uses fast-check with `node:test`. Both implementations are loaded in one process:
+
+- OLD: `test/old-rates.js`, a frozen copy of the original `src/rates.js` (it requires `request`).
+- NEW: `src/rates.js`.
+
+For each API from the inventory (`getRate`, `archiveReceipt`) the test:
+
+1. generates a case: the call arguments (`from`/`to`, payload, `timeoutMs`) and the server's answer (status 200 to 599, body kind, content type, delay before the headers, delay before the body, optional `Location`);
+2. starts two local `node:http` servers (one per implementation) that answer the case identically and record every request they receive;
+3. calls OLD and NEW with the same arguments;
+4. compares the resolved value or the kind of rejection, and the recorded requests (method, path with raw query string, headers, body);
+5. fails on any difference and prints the shrunk case and both observations.
+
+Headers that the HTTP client adds by itself and that the project neither sets nor reads are listed in `IGNORED_HEADERS` in the test (see "Known behavioural differences"). Everything else is compared.
+
+A second test, `séance harness: request vs request`, runs OLD against OLD and must pass; it proves the harness is deterministic.
+
+Timing: the generator picks each delay clearly below or clearly above `timeoutMs` (margin 60 ms). Outcomes inside the margin depend on event-loop jitter in either implementation, so they are not generated.
+
+While `request` is installed it is the oracle. `test/seance.request.golden.json` records OLD observations for a fixed set of generated cases (`node test/record-seance-golden.js`, run before removal). After `request` is removed the live test is skipped and the golden replay keeps guarding the new code.
+
+## Risks
+
+- A behaviour that the inventory missed. The test only covers the APIs it lists.
+- Differences the project relies on by accident (error messages, key order, empty body is `undefined`). Decide per case whether to match or to change the caller.
+- `timeout` meaning: `request` uses an idle timeout, `AbortSignal.timeout` is a total deadline. The new code needs an idle timer to match.
+
+## Order of work
+
+Inventory, then the séance against the old code, then the switch, then the removal.
+
+## Known behavioural differences
+
+(filled in after the séance has run against the new code)
