+# Requirements: Move off request
+
+Generated by Necromancer (engine: engine). `request` is **zombie**; the chosen successor is `fetch` (native).
+
+## Why
+
+- Evidence: Deprecated on npm: "request has been deprecated, see https://github.com/request/request/issues/3142" (npm)
+- Evidence: Abandoned: the latest release (2.88.2) is 6.7 years old and nothing shows it is still looked after (low OpenSSF Maintained score). (npm)
+- Evidence: The latest release (2.88.2) is 6.7 years old. (npm)
+- Evidence: OpenSSF Scorecard rates maintenance 0 out of 10. (scorecard)
+- Evidence: 1 known advisory affects version 2.88.2: GHSA-p8p7-x288-28g6. (osv)
+
+Why this successor: Node 18+ and every browser ship fetch, so most uses need no package.
+This project declares `"engines": { "node": ">=18" }` in `package.json`, so `fetch` is available everywhere it runs.
+
+## R1. Know how the project uses the dependency
+
+**User story.** As a maintainer, I want a list of every way the project uses `request`, so that the migration covers all of it and nothing more.
+
+- R1.1 WHEN the project is searched, THE migration SHALL list every import, require and call of `request` with the exported names used.
+- R1.2 THE list SHALL be saved in the repository so reviewers can check it against the diff.
+
+Result (saved in `docs/migration/request-usage.md`): one `require('request')` in `src/rates.js:3`, two calls, `request(options, cb)` in `getRate` (`src/rates.js:14`) and `request.post(options, cb)` in `archiveReceipt` (`src/rates.js:42`). No other file imports it.
+
+## R2. Same behaviour before and after
+
+**User story.** As a maintainer, I want proof that `fetch` answers like `request` for the way the project uses it, so that the switch is safe.
+
+- R2.1 THE project SHALL have a differential property-based test (a séance) that asks `request` and `fetch` the same generated questions over each API in R1.1.
+- R2.2 WHEN the two answers differ, THE test SHALL fail and print the input and both answers.
+- R2.3 WHERE both throw, THE test SHALL compare the kind of error, not the message text.
+- R2.4 THE test SHALL pass with the old implementation on both sides before any code is changed.
+- R2.5 THE séance SHALL compare, for `getRate` and `archiveReceipt`: the resolved value or the kind of rejection, the HTTP status, the parsed body, and every request the server received (method, path with query string, headers, body).
+- R2.6 THE séance generators SHALL cover the domain the project really uses: query values that are empty, contain spaces, unicode and characters that need escaping; statuses 200 to 599; JSON, non-JSON and empty bodies; any content type; slow answers against a short `timeoutMs`.
+- R2.7 AFTER `request` is removed, THE séance SHALL keep guarding the new code by replaying recorded `request` observations (a golden file) through `npm test`.
+
+## R3. Use the platform instead
+
+**User story.** As a maintainer, I want the code to call `fetch` directly, so that the project carries no package for it.
+
+- R3.1 THE code SHALL call `fetch` where it called `request`.
+- R3.2 THE project's supported runtimes (Node version, browser list) SHALL all provide `fetch`.
+
+## R4. Remove the dead dependency
+
+**User story.** As a maintainer, I want `request` gone, so that nothing unmaintained stays in the tree.
+
+- R4.1 THE project SHALL NOT declare `request` in any manifest or lockfile after the migration.
+- R4.2 THE build, type check and test suite SHALL pass after the migration.
+- R4.3 THE dependency audit SHALL no longer report GHSA-p8p7-x288-28g6 for this project.
+
+## R5. Safe to undo
+
+**User story.** As a maintainer, I want each step in its own commit, so that I can revert one step without losing the others.
+
+- R5.1 THE migration SHALL be made in small commits: test first, then the switch, then the removal.
