+# How the project uses `request`
+
+Found with `grep -rnE "require\(['\"]request['\"]).*|\brequest(\.[a-z]+)?\(" src test` (requirement R1.1).
+
+| Where | Form | Call |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `src/rates.js:3` | `const request = require('request')` | the only import |
+| `src/rates.js:14` | `request(options, cb)` in `getRate` | GET `{baseUrl}/latest`, options `url`, `qs {base, symbols}`, `headers {User-Agent, Accept}`, `json: true`, `timeout` |
+| `src/rates.js:42` | `request.post(options, cb)` in `archiveReceipt` | POST `{baseUrl}/receipts`, options `url`, `headers {User-Agent}`, `json: payload`, `timeout` |
+
+`src/index.js` and `src/receipt.js` do not import `request`; `src/index.js` reaches it through `getRate` and `archiveReceipt`.
+No other option is used: no `auth`, `form`, `formData`, `proxy`, `jar`, `gzip`, `encoding`, `followRedirect`, streams or `pipe`.
+
+## Behaviour of `request` the project relies on, knowingly or not
+
+- `qs` is serialised with the `qs` package (RFC 3986 percent-encoding, a space becomes `%20`).
+- `json: true` parses the response body with `JSON.parse` whatever the content type; on a parse error the body stays the raw string; an empty body stays `undefined`.
+- `json: <object>` sends `JSON.stringify(object)` with `content-type: application/json` and also adds `accept: application/json`.
+- `timeout` is an idle timeout on the socket (connect, then each gap in the answer), not a total deadline.
+- A 3xx answer with a `Location` header is followed for GET (any 3xx status) and not followed for POST.
+- Any HTTP status resolves; only network errors and timeouts reject.
-- 
