 ## Known behavioural differences
 
-(filled in after the séance has run against the new code)
+The séance found these. Differences 1 to 4 were matched in `src/rates.js` so that the séance passes without narrowing. Differences 5 to 11 cannot be matched with `fetch` or are not a contract, so the séance generator excludes them and a test named `séance: known difference, ...` pins the new behaviour where it can be pinned.
+
+Matched in the new code (found by the séance, fixed):
+
+1. Timeout. `request` uses an idle timeout (connect, then each gap in the answer). `AbortSignal.timeout` is a total deadline and also ended a slow body as "no rate" because the error was swallowed. `send()` in `src/rates.js` restarts a timer after the headers and after every chunk. First counterexample: `timeoutMs 122, headDelay 59, bodyDelay 56`, `request` resolved the rate, `fetch` rejected.
+2. `Accept` on POST. `request` adds `accept: application/json` when `json` is set; `fetch` sent `*/*`. The new code sets it.
+3. Redirects. `request` follows a 3xx answer that has a `Location` header for GET (any 3xx status, up to 10 times, with `Referer` set to the previous address) and never for POST. `fetch` follows only 301, 302, 303, 307, 308 and also follows a POST. The new code uses `redirect: 'manual'` and follows by hand under the old rules.
+4. Query encoding. `qs` writes a space as `%20` and escapes `! ' ( ) *`; `URLSearchParams` writes `+` and leaves `* ` alone. The new code uses `encodeURIComponent` plus the five characters.
+
+Accepted (generator narrowed, each one stated in `test/seance.request.test.js`):
+
+5. A lone UTF-16 surrogate in a query value. `qs` 6.5.5 sends corrupt bytes and swallows the next character (`'\ud800'` becomes `%F0%90%80%80`, `'a\udc00b'` becomes `a%F0%90%81%A2`). The new code sends U+FFFD (`%EF%BF%BD`). Reproducing a data-corrupting bug is wrong. Query values come from well-formed strings; `séance: known difference, a lone surrogate ...` pins the new bytes.
+6. A 407 answer. The fetch standard turns 407 into a network error (`TypeError: fetch failed`, no status). `request` gave `rates service answered 407` from `getRate` and `{ status: 407 }` from `archiveReceipt`. Both now reject. The generator does not produce 407; `séance: known difference, a 407 answer ...` pins it. A rates service is not a proxy, so this is unlikely to matter.
+7. A 205 answer with content. `fetch` treats 205 like 204 and 304 (null body, settles on the headers) and returns `body: undefined`; `request` waits for and returns the content. RFC 9110 forbids content in a 205. Every generated 205 has an empty body and no body delay; pinned by `séance: known difference, fetch ignores the content of a 205 answer`.
+8. A 304 answer with `Location` that the server keeps open for longer than `timeoutMs`. `request` times out after following the redirect (an accident of its timer handling; 300, 301, 302, 303 and 307 do not). The new code follows the redirect and succeeds. Such a 304 is generated without a body delay.
+9. Request head size. A request head over 16 KiB is refused (431) by Node's HTTP server; `fetch` sends about 100 bytes more headers than `request`, so a query within about 100 bytes of the limit passes with `request` and fails with `fetch`. Generated long values stay under about 12 KB.
+10. Headers added by the HTTP client itself are not compared: `host` (different port per test server), `connection`, `keep-alive`, `accept-encoding`, `accept-language`, `sec-fetch-mode`. The project sets and reads none of them; every other header, including `user-agent`, `accept`, `content-type`, `content-length` and `referer`, is compared.
+11. The error objects. A timeout is a `TimeoutError` with `code: 'ETIMEDOUT'` (`request`: `ESOCKETTIMEDOUT` for a slow answer); a cut or refused connection is a `TypeError: fetch failed` with the Node error as `cause` (`request`: the Node error itself). The séance compares the kind (`timeout`, `network`, or the project's own two messages), as the skill prescribes. No code in the project reads `err.code`.
+
+Not covered by the séance: non-string query values (the project passes strings), `baseUrl` values other than `http://host:port`, HTTPS, proxies and environment proxy variables, response compression, and timing within 60 ms of `timeoutMs` (decided by event-loop jitter in either implementation).
